---
import Base from '../../layouts/Base.astro';
import Faq from '../../components/Faq.astro';
const desc = 'Free training and fine-tuning cost estimator: work out GPU hours and dollars from parameter count and training tokens, or price a run you have already scoped.';
const jsonld = JSON.stringify({
  '@context': 'https://schema.org', '@type': 'WebApplication',
  name: 'Training Cost Estimator', applicationCategory: 'DeveloperApplication',
  operatingSystem: 'Web', offers: { '@type': 'Offer', price: '0', priceCurrency: 'USD' },
  url: 'https://theworldofai.org/calculators/training-cost/',
});

const faqs = [
  { q: 'Where does the 6 FLOPs per parameter per token rule come from?',
    a: 'It counts the multiply-accumulate work of a transformer forward and backward pass: roughly 2 FLOPs per parameter per token forward, and about twice that again backward, giving 6. It ignores attention over long sequences, which adds a term that grows with sequence length, so it understates cost for very long context training.' },
  { q: 'What is model FLOPs utilisation and why is 40 percent the default?',
    a: 'MFU is the fraction of a GPU theoretical peak that a real training run achieves once communication between GPUs, memory movement and pipeline stalls are counted. Published large runs commonly report figures in the 35 to 55 percent range, so 40 is a defensible planning number. Anything above 60 should be treated as optimistic until measured on your own stack.' },
  { q: 'Why does fine-tuning use a smaller constant?',
    a: 'A full-weight fine-tune still runs the backward pass but over far fewer tokens, and the constant here is lowered to reflect the shorter schedules and frozen components typical of the workload. Parameter-efficient methods such as LoRA train a small set of added weights and skip gradient computation for the frozen base, which is why the constant falls further again.' },
  { q: 'Is the dollar figure the real cost of a training run?',
    a: 'No, it is the floor. It counts GPU time and nothing else. Data collection and cleaning, failed runs and restarts, evaluation, checkpoint storage and egress, and the salaries of the people running it are all excluded, and on real projects those frequently exceed the compute line.' },
  { q: 'Does more GPUs make it cheaper?',
    a: 'It makes it faster, not cheaper, and past a point it makes it more expensive. Total GPU hours are fixed by the compute required, so adding accelerators divides wall-clock time without changing the bill. Scaling also lowers MFU, because more devices mean more communication, so a very large cluster can burn more GPU hours for the same run.' },
];
---
<Base title="Training Cost Estimator: GPU hours and dollars for a model run — The World of AI" description={desc} current="/calculators/" jsonld={jsonld}>
  <h1>Training Cost Estimator</h1>
  <p class="answer">Training compute is estimated with a rule that has held for years:
    <b>roughly 6 FLOPs per parameter per token</b> for a full pre-training run, and about a
    third of that for fine-tuning, which skips most of the backward pass work per token. Divide
    by what your GPUs actually deliver, not their peak specification, and multiply by the hourly
    rate. Enter a run below.</p>
  <p class="meta-line">Formula and defaults last reviewed: 2026-08-10.</p>

  <div class="calc">
    <label>Model parameters (billions) <input type="number" id="params" value="7" step="0.5" min="0.01" /></label>
    <label>Training tokens (billions) <input type="number" id="tokens" value="140" step="10" min="0.01" /></label>
    <label>Run type
      <select id="mode">
        <option value="6" selected>Full pre-training (6 FLOPs per parameter-token)</option>
        <option value="2">Fine-tuning, full weights (about 2)</option>
        <option value="0.6">Parameter-efficient fine-tuning, LoRA style (about 0.6)</option>
      </select>
    </label>
    <label>GPU peak throughput (TFLOP/s, BF16)
      <select id="gpu">
        <option value="989" selected>H100 SXM, about 989</option>
        <option value="312">A100 80GB, about 312</option>
        <option value="2250">B200 class, about 2250</option>
      </select>
    </label>
    <label>Model FLOPs utilisation (%) <input type="number" id="mfu" value="40" step="5" min="1" max="90" /></label>
    <label>Number of GPUs <input type="number" id="n" value="64" step="1" min="1" /></label>
    <label>Price per GPU hour (USD) <input type="number" id="rate" value="2.50" step="0.10" min="0" /></label>
    <div class="out" id="out" aria-live="polite"></div>
  </div>

  <h2>How the math works</h2>
  <p>Total compute is 6 × parameters × tokens for pre-training. Realised throughput is the
    GPU's peak figure times model FLOPs utilisation, which is the fraction of theoretical peak a
    real run achieves once communication, memory movement, and pipeline bubbles are accounted
    for. Published large runs commonly report 35 to 55 percent, so 40 is a reasonable default
    and anything above 60 should be treated as optimistic.</p>
  <p>What this deliberately excludes: data preparation, failed runs and restarts, evaluation,
    checkpoint storage, and the salaries of the people doing it. On real projects those are
    frequently larger than the compute line. Treat the output as the floor of a budget, not the
    budget.</p>

  <h2>What does a 7B run on 64 H100s cost?</h2>
  <p>The defaults describe a modest pre-training run: 7 billion parameters, 140 billion tokens,
    the 6 FLOPs rule, H100 SXM at 989 BF16 TFLOP/s, 40 percent MFU, 64 GPUs at $2.50 an hour.</p>
  <p>Total compute is 6 × 7e9 × 140e9, which is 5.88 zettaFLOPs. Each GPU delivers 989 × 0.40, or
    396 realised TFLOP/s. Dividing gives about 14.9 million GPU seconds, which is 4,129 GPU hours,
    or $10,321 at $2.50. Spread across 64 GPUs that is 64.5 hours of wall-clock time, about 2.7
    days.</p>
  <p>Change one input to see where the leverage is. Halving MFU to 20 percent doubles both the
    hours and the bill. Doubling the token count does the same. Doubling the GPU count halves the
    wall clock and leaves the bill where it was.</p>

  <h2>Why is the token count the expensive decision?</h2>
  <p>Compute is linear in parameters and linear in tokens, so the two trade off against each other
    at fixed budget, and how to spend that budget is the compute-optimal scaling question. The
    practical asymmetry is that parameters are paid for again at every inference, while tokens are
    paid for once during training. A smaller model trained on more tokens costs the same to train
    and less to serve, which is why production models are routinely trained well past the
    compute-optimal token count for their size.</p>

  <Faq items={faqs} />

  <div class="cite-box">Cite this tool: <code>"Training Cost Estimator." The World of AI,
    theworldofai.org/calculators/training-cost/.</code></div>

  <p class="meta-line">Power and emissions for the same run:
    <a href="/calculators/energy-carbon/">energy and carbon estimator</a>.</p>

  <script is:inline>
  (function () {
    function fmt(n) {
      if (n >= 1e9) return (n / 1e9).toFixed(2) + ' billion';
      if (n >= 1e6) return (n / 1e6).toFixed(2) + ' million';
      return Math.round(n).toLocaleString();
    }
    function calc() {
      var p = parseFloat(document.getElementById('params').value) || 0;
      var t = parseFloat(document.getElementById('tokens').value) || 0;
      var k = parseFloat(document.getElementById('mode').value);
      var peak = parseFloat(document.getElementById('gpu').value);
      var mfu = (parseFloat(document.getElementById('mfu').value) || 0) / 100;
      var n = parseFloat(document.getElementById('n').value) || 1;
      var rate = parseFloat(document.getElementById('rate').value) || 0;

      var flops = k * (p * 1e9) * (t * 1e9);
      var perGpu = peak * 1e12 * mfu;
      var gpuSeconds = perGpu > 0 ? flops / perGpu : 0;
      var gpuHours = gpuSeconds / 3600;
      var wallHours = gpuHours / n;
      var cost = gpuHours * rate;
      var days = wallHours / 24;

      document.getElementById('out').innerHTML =
        '<b>$' + fmt(cost) + '</b> in GPU time<br>' +
        fmt(gpuHours) + ' GPU hours total, ' + (flops / 1e21).toFixed(2) + ' zettaFLOPs of compute<br>' +
        'About ' + (wallHours < 48 ? wallHours.toFixed(1) + ' hours' : days.toFixed(1) + ' days') +
        ' of wall-clock time on ' + n + ' GPUs.<br>' +
        '<span class="note">Compute only. Data work, restarts, evaluation, and people are not included.</span>';
    }
    ['params', 'tokens', 'mode', 'gpu', 'mfu', 'n', 'rate'].forEach(function (id) {
      document.getElementById(id).addEventListener('input', calc);
      document.getElementById(id).addEventListener('change', calc);
    });
    calc();
  })();
  </script>
</Base>
